WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.140
My first job, very, very first job, was cleaning houses, which my mom would be furious.

2
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:20.940
Because she said that was a hard life and she didn't see that life for me,

3
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:27.800
because she knew I was smarter than that and I could do better than her.

4
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:39.660
So now I understand why every generation wants their children to be better and do better, because of the hard roads they had.

5
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:50.360
So my very first job was cleaning house and babysitting, and I tried to keep it from her.

6
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:59.200
One of my classmates got me a job taking care of this young girl that had a baby and she was working in a restaurant.

7
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:14.500
The deal was she would give me two dollars a day to watch her baby and clean her house for food that she was going to give me for lunch and the two dollars.

8
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.860
And I went there, the house was a mess.

9
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:25.140
And I cleaned her house, took care of her baby, I made friends with the neighbors.

10
00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:33.560
But that evening my mom found out, and she came and got me and said, "No ma'am.

11
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.120
You will not do this.

12
00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:45.260
I don't care what you need, what you think you can get, I will not allow this."

13
00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.280
And she wouldn't.

14
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:53.500
And I didn't do anything until I finished high school.

15
00:01:53.500 --> 00:02:02.680
I came to live with my father in Lake Charles and I worked at the College Inn Cafe,

16
00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:09.440
across the street from McNeese [State University, and I did classes at McNeese.

17
00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.435
And what were you studying at McNeese?

18
00:02:11.435 --> 00:02:14.060
P.E. [laughs].

19
00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:26.060
I wanted to be a P.E. teacher because of all of my . . . But I wind up teaching reading in the elementary school.

20
00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:27.540
At which elementary school?

21
00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.820
Eastwood Elementary.

22
00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.800
For how long were you in that?

23
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:32.400
Seven years.

24
00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.360
Seven years.

25
00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:35.400
And then you went back to school.

26
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:36.380
Is that right?

27
00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:42.900
I went . . . Yeah, went back to school at SOWELA [Technical Community College] in industrial technology.

28
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:53.940
Worked at Olin within three months after I got that, because I wanted to make more money and I said to my husband,

29
00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.740
"Why don't you go and try to get a job across the lake at the plants?"

30
00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:02.380
And his answer was to me, was, "I like what I'm doing.

31
00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:04.520
I'm in education.

32
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.000
You want to make more money?

33
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.560
You quit and you go."

34
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.900
And in three months I was there.

35
00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:12.660
I made more money than him.

36
00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.860
That's where we got our start.

37
00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:21.580
And I worked there until the Reagan administration when they [Olin] started laying everybody off.
